## Why This Is Asked
This problem tests a sophisticated dynamic programming variant where the non-adjacency constraint introduces complexity beyond standard interval selection problems. It requires careful state design and transition logic to maintain separation between blocks.

## Key Concepts
- Dynamic programming with separation constraints between selected intervals
- Efficient computation of range maxima for subarray value calculation
- Backpointer implementation for reconstructing optimal block endpoints
- Time-space trade-off analysis: O(n²k) time complexity with O(nk) space when using precomputed range maximum queries

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxBlocks(A, k) {
  const n = A.length;
  const dp = Array(n+1).fill().map(() => Array(k+1).fill(-Infinity));
  const prev = Array(n+1).fill().map(() => Array(k+1).fill(-1));

  // Precompute range maxima
  const rangeMax = Array(n).fill().map(() => Array(n).fill(0));
  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    let currentMax = A[i];
    for (let j = i; j < n; j++) {
      currentMax = Math.max(currentMax, A[j]);
      rangeMax[i][j] = currentMax;
    }
  }

  // Base case: 0 blocks
  for (let i = 0; i <= n; i++) dp[i][0] = 0;

  // Fill DP table
  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    for (let t = 1; t <= k; t++) {
      // Skip i
      if (dp[i-1][t] > dp[i][t]) {
        dp[i][t] = dp[i-1][t];
      }

      // End block at i
      for (let s = 1; s <= i-1; s++) {
        const prevIdx = s-2 >= 0 ? s-2 : 0;
        if (dp[prevIdx][t-1] !== -Infinity) {
          const val = rangeMax[s-1][i-1] + dp[prevIdx][t-1];
          if (val > dp[i][t]) {
            dp[i][t] = val;
            prev[i][t] = s-1;
          }
        }
      }
    }
  }

  return { maxValue: dp[n][k], reconstruct: () => reconstructBlocks(prev, n, k) };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How can we optimize the time complexity to O(nk) using a monotonic queue or segment tree structure?
- How does the solution adapt to different indexing conventions (1-based vs 0-based) and handle edge cases where n < 2k-1?
- What modifications are needed if we allow adjacent blocks or change the objective function?

## Why This Is Asked

This question tests your ability to design a dynamic programming solution for fixed-partition subproblems. It requires careful state definition, transition logic, and the ability to handle substring-to-integer conversion efficiently. Additionally, storing cut pointers enables reconstruction of the optimal partition.

## Key Concepts

- **Partition DP**: Divide a linear sequence into exactly k segments
- **On-the-fly value computation**: Accumulate digit values incrementally without re-parsing substrings
- **Reconstruction**: Maintain a parent pointer array to recover the cut positions
- **Time/Space tradeoffs**: O(n²k) time complexity with O(nk) space; can optimize to O(nk) space using rolling arrays

## Code Example

```javascript
function maxSumPartition(s, k) {
  const n = s.length;
  const digits = s.split('').map(Number);

  const dp = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(-Infinity));
  const cut = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(0));

  dp[0][0] = 0;

  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    for (let t = 1; t <= Math.min(i, k); t++) {
      let val = 0;
      for (let j = i; j >= t; j--) {
        val = digits[j - 1] * Math.pow(10, i - j) + val;
        if (dp[j - 1][t - 1] !== -Infinity) {
          const sum = dp[j - 1][t - 1] + val;
          if (sum > dp[i][t]) {
            dp[i][t] = sum;
            cut[i][t] = j - 1;
          }
        }
      }
    }
  }

  const cuts = [];
  let pos = n;
  for (let t = k; t >= 1; t--) {
    cuts.push(cut[pos][t]);
    pos = cut[pos][t];
  }

  return { maxSum: dp[n][k], cutPositions: cuts.reverse() };
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

This question tests a concrete DP pattern for selecting exactly k non-overlapping subarrays with a fixed per-subarray cost. It requires careful state definitions, reconstruction, and edge-case handling of negative sums.

## Key Concepts

- DP for k disjoint subarrays with end-state tracking
- Prefix sums or running sums to compute subarray value fast
- Reconstruction pointers to recover endpoints
- Time: O(nk); Space: O(nk) (with optimizations)

## Code Example

```javascript
function maxKSubarrays(A, k, c) {
  const n = A.length, NEG = -1e15;
  const dp = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => Array(k+1).fill(NEG));
  const end = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => Array(k+1).fill(NEG));
  dp[0][0] = 0;
  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    for (let t = 0; t <= k; t++) {
      end[i][t] = NEG;
      if (t > 0) end[i][t] = Math.max(end[i-1][t] + A[i-1], dp[i-1][t-1] + A[i-1] - c);
      dp[i][t] = Math.max(dp[i-1][t], end[i][t]);
    }
  }
  return dp[n][k];
}
```

