- How handle cases where all values are negative or c is large?

## Why This Is Asked
This problem extends classic knapsack-style dynamic programming to incorporate time-based constraints (deadlines) and a limit on the number of selectable jobs. It tests understanding of multi-dimensional DP states, proper handling of time as a resource, and the ability to reconstruct the solution.

## Key Concepts
- Scheduling with deadlines and profits
- 3D DP formulation tracking (jobs processed, jobs selected, time)
- Backward iteration to avoid reusing the same job
- Reconstruction via predecessor pointers
- Time complexity: O(n × K × D), where D is the maximum deadline

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxProfitJobs(jobs, K) {
  jobs.sort((a, b) => a.d - b.d);
  const maxT = Math.max(...jobs.map(j => j.d));
  const NEG = -Infinity;
  const dp = Array.from({ length: K + 1 }, () => Array(maxT + 1).fill(NEG));
  const prev = Array.from({ length: K + 1 }, () => Array(maxT + 1).fill(null));

  dp[0][0] = 0;

  for (const { p, d, w } of jobs) {
    for (let k = K - 1; k >= 0; k--) {
      for (let t = d; t >= p; t--) {
        if (dp[k][t - p] !== NEG) {
          const newProfit = dp[k][t - p] + w;
          if (newProfit > dp[k + 1][t]) {
            dp[k + 1][t] = newProfit;
            prev[k + 1][t] = { prevK: k, prevT: t - p };
          }
        }
      }
    }
  }

  let bestProfit = 0, bestK = 0, bestT = 0;
  for (let k = 1; k <= K; k++) {
    for (let t = 0; t <= maxT; t++) {
      if (dp[k][t] > bestProfit) {
        bestProfit = dp[k][t];
        bestK = k;
        bestT = t;
      }
    }
  }

  const selected = [];
  let [k, t] = [bestK, bestT];
  while (k > 0) {
    const { prevK, prevT } = prev[k][t];
    selected.push({ finishTime: t });
    k = prevK;
    t = prevT;
  }

  return { profit: bestProfit, jobs: selected.reverse() };
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests DP with partitioning and a nontrivial per-substring cost, plus backtracking to recover partitions.

## Key Concepts
- DP over partitions: dp[i][t]
- Precompute cost[l][r] in O(n^2) via two-pointer mismatches
- Backpointers for reconstruction
- Complexity: O(n^2 k) time, O(n^2) space

## Code Example
```javascript
function minCostPalindromePartitions(s, k) {
  const n = s.length;
  const cost = Array.from({length: n}, ()=> Array(n).fill(0));
  for (let l = 0; l < n; l++) {
    for (let r = l; r < n; r++) {
      let a = l, b = r, c = 0;
      while (a < b) { if (s[a] !== s[b]) c++; a++; b--; }
      cost[l][r] = c;
    }
  }
  const dp = Array.from({length: n}, () => Array(k + 1).fill(Infinity));
  const parent = Array.from({length: n}, () => Array(k + 1).fill(-1));
  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) dp[i][1] = cost[0][i];
  for (let t = 2; t <= k; t++) {
    for (let i = t - 1; i < n; i++) {
      for (let p = t - 2; p < i; p++) {
        const val = dp[p][t - 1] + cost[p + 1][i];
        if (val < dp[i][t]) { dp[i][t] = val; parent[i][t] = p; }
      }
    }
  }
  const splits = [];
  let i = n - 1, t = k;
  while (t >= 1) {
    splits.push(i);
    i = parent[i][t];
    t--;
  }
  splits.reverse();
  return { minCost: dp[n - 1][k], partitions: splits };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you optimize space for large n or small k?
- How would this adapt if cost used a different per-substring metric?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests a novel fixed-gap non-crossing pairing constraint, a natural extension of classic non-crossing matching problems, while probing DP state design and reconstruction.

## Key Concepts
- DP over prefixes with fixed pair distance
- Non-crossing/disjoint constraint handling
- Backpointers for reconstruction
- Space/time optimization trade-offs

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxFixedGapPairs(A, d, k) {
  const n = A.length;
  const dp = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(-Infinity));
  const take = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(false));
  dp[0][0] = 0;
  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    for (let t = 0; t <= k; t++) {
      dp[i][t] = dp[i - 1][t];
      if (t > 0 && i - d - 1 >= 0) {
        const cand = dp[i - d - 1][t - 1] + A[i - 1] * A[i - d - 1];
        if (cand > dp[i][t]) {
          dp[i][t] = cand;
          take[i][t] = true;
        }
      }
    }
  }
  const pairs = [];
  let i = n, t = k;
  while (t > 0 && i > 0) {
    if (take[i][t]) {
      pairs.push([i - d, i]); // 1-based indices of the pair
      i = i - d - 1;
      t--;
    } else {
      i--;
    }
  }
  pairs.reverse();
  return { value: dp[n][k], pairs };
}
```

## Why This Problem Is Asked

This question tests dynamic programming on partitioning with a non-linear cost function (block maximum). It extends beyond standard max-sum variants and evaluates careful indexing, prefix handling, and solution reconstruction.

## Key Concepts

- **Contiguous partition DP**: State dp[i][t] represents the minimum cost for the prefix of length i using exactly t blocks
- **Transition**: Computes the running maximum for the last block while finding the optimal split point
- **Reconstruction**: Backtracking through the DP table to determine block endpoints

## Code Example

```javascript
function minSumOfBlockMax(A, k) {
  const n = A.length;
  const dp = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(Infinity));
  const split = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(0));

  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    dp[i][1] = Math.max(...A.slice(0, i));
  }

  for (let t = 2; t <= k; t++) {
    for (let i = t; i <= n; i++) {
      let maxVal = 0;
      for (let j = i - 1; j >= t - 1; j--) {
        maxVal = Math.max(maxVal, A[j]);
        const cost = dp[j][t - 1] + maxVal;
        if (cost < dp[i][t]) {
          dp[i][t] = cost;
          split[i][t] = j;
        }
      }
    }
  }

  const blocks = [];
  let i = n, t = k;
  while (t > 0) {
    const j = split[i][t];
    blocks.unshift({ start: j + 1, end: i });
    i = j;
    t--;
  }

  return { minSum: dp[n][k], blocks };
}
```

